precept TeamInviteCampaign

# A B2B "invite your team" campaign. An organization launches a campaign
# bounded to one corporate email domain; the campaign owner invites
# colleagues by email, recipients accept, and the campaign closes when
# enough seats are filled.
#
# This precept is the corpus's canonical case-insensitive showcase. Email
# addresses, promo codes, department codes, license-plate numbers — the
# domains where case-mismatch bugs hide — share one pattern: two values
# that differ only in casing must be the same value. In case-sensitive
# systems, this is the bug that creates duplicate accounts, lets a coupon
# get redeemed twice, and makes "Alice@…" and "alice@…" look like two
# different people. Precept eliminates the bug structurally: every CI
# field is declared `~string`, the type checker refuses `==` / `!=` on
# those fields, sets and lookups key with `OrdinalIgnoreCase` so casing
# never produces duplicates, and `~startsWith` / `~endsWith` give the
# function-form CI surface for prefix and suffix tests.
#
# The flows below exercise all four CI surfaces in one place:
#   • scalar `~=` and `!~`   — code match, owner self-invite guard
#   • `~endsWith(...)`       — corporate-domain restriction on invites
#   • `set of ~string`       — duplicate-invitation prevention
#   • `lookup of ~string`    — per-invitee timestamps and roles

# ── Identity ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
field CampaignId as string notempty maxlength 50
field CampaignName as string notempty maxlength 200

# The corporate domain that bounds the campaign. Stored `~string` because
# administrators may enter "Acme.com" while invitees type "acme.com" —
# both are the same domain. The `~endsWith` guard on Invite reads from
# this field with case-insensitive semantics.
field OrganizationDomain as ~string notempty maxlength 100

# The campaign owner. Receives bounce notifications and is barred from
# inviting themselves. The `!~` comparison on Invite uses this field; if
# OwnerEmail were plain `string`, an author could write `Invite.Email !=
# OwnerEmail` and the bug would slip through whenever the email casings
# disagreed.
field OwnerEmail as ~string notempty maxlength 200

# Optional public-facing code customers type to claim a seat. Compared
# with `~=` below — "ACME-2026" and "acme-2026" both redeem.
field InviteCode as ~string optional maxlength 50

# Membership and timing. Two `set of ~string` and one `lookup of ~string`
# carry every email reference in the campaign. The CI element comparer
# means a re-invite of the same person with a different casing collapses
# to one entry, and the `contains` guards below fire correctly regardless
# of how the email was capitalized.
field InvitedEmails as set of ~string
field AcceptedEmails as set of ~string
field InviteSentAt as lookup of ~string to instant

field SeatTarget as integer positive max 1000
field InviteCount as integer default 0 nonnegative
field AcceptedCount as integer default 0 nonnegative

field Log as log of string
field CreatedAt as instant
field LaunchedAt as instant optional
field ClosedAt as instant optional

rule InviteCount <= SeatTarget * 3 because "Campaigns may invite up to three times the seat target ({SeatTarget}); higher fan-out should split across campaigns"
rule AcceptedCount <= InviteCount because "Acceptances cannot exceed invitations sent"

# ── Lifecycle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
state Draft initial
state Running
state Closed terminal

in Running ensure LaunchedAt is set because "A running campaign must record when it was launched"
in Closed ensure ClosedAt is set because "A closed campaign must record when it was closed"

# ── Events ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
event Create(
    CampaignId as string notempty maxlength 50,
    CampaignName as string notempty maxlength 200,
    OrganizationDomain as ~string notempty maxlength 100,
    OwnerEmail as ~string notempty maxlength 200,
    InviteCode as ~string optional maxlength 50,
    SeatTarget as integer positive max 1000) initial

event Invite(Email as ~string notempty maxlength 200)
event Accept(Email as ~string notempty maxlength 200, CodeEntered as ~string optional maxlength 50)
event Launch
event Close

# ── Event-arg preconditions ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `~endsWith` is the function-form CI surface. Without it, an author who
# needed case-insensitive suffix matching would have to write something
# like `endsWith(toLower(Invite.Email), toLower("@" + OrganizationDomain))`
# — easy to forget, hard to read, and the bug only surfaces when a
# customer reports it. The `~` prefix declares intent.
on Invite ensure ~endsWith(Invite.Email, "@" + OrganizationDomain) because "Invitation address {Invite.Email} is outside the organization domain {OrganizationDomain}"

# Owner self-invite — declaratively rejected. `!~` is required because
# OwnerEmail is `~string`; `!=` here is a compile error, which means the
# author cannot accidentally make this guard case-sensitive.
on Invite ensure Invite.Email !~ OwnerEmail because "The campaign owner cannot invite themselves to the campaign"

# ── Construction ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
on Create
    -> set CampaignId = Create.CampaignId
    -> set CampaignName = Create.CampaignName
    -> set OrganizationDomain = Create.OrganizationDomain
    -> set OwnerEmail = Create.OwnerEmail
    -> set InviteCode = Create.InviteCode
    -> set SeatTarget = Create.SeatTarget
    -> set CreatedAt = now()
    -> append Log "Campaign {Create.CampaignId} drafted for {Create.OrganizationDomain} (target {Create.SeatTarget} seats)"

# ── Entry hooks ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
to Running -> set LaunchedAt = now()
to Closed -> set ClosedAt = now()

from Draft on Launch
    -> append Log "Campaign launched"
    -> transition Running

# ── Invite flow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `InvitedEmails contains Invite.Email` runs the set's OrdinalIgnoreCase
# comparer, so a caller who first invited "Alice@acme.com" and then tries
# again as "alice@acme.com" or "ALICE@ACME.COM" is rejected here — before
# the counter increments, before the lookup tombstone is written. That is
# the case-mismatch bug, structurally eliminated.
from Running on Invite when InvitedEmails contains Invite.Email
    -> reject "Email {Invite.Email} has already been invited to this campaign"
from Running on Invite
    -> add InvitedEmails Invite.Email
    -> put InviteSentAt Invite.Email = now()
    -> set InviteCount = InviteCount + 1
    -> append Log "Invited {Invite.Email}"
    -> no transition

# ── Accept flow ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two CI guards stack here. First: the optional invite code must match
# the campaign's stored InviteCode if one was set — `~=` makes this
# case-insensitive without the author having to normalize either side.
# Second: the inviter's casing of the email may not match what the
# invitee typed at accept time; the `contains` check resolves both casings
# to the same set element.
from Running on Accept when InviteCode is set and Accept.CodeEntered is not set
    -> reject "This campaign requires an invite code"
from Running on Accept when InviteCode is set and Accept.CodeEntered !~ InviteCode
    -> reject "Invite code {Accept.CodeEntered} does not match the campaign's code"
from Running on Accept when not (InvitedEmails contains Accept.Email)
    -> reject "Email {Accept.Email} was never invited to this campaign"
from Running on Accept when AcceptedEmails contains Accept.Email
    -> reject "Email {Accept.Email} has already accepted this invitation"
from Running on Accept
    -> add AcceptedEmails Accept.Email
    -> set AcceptedCount = AcceptedCount + 1
    -> append Log "Accepted by {Accept.Email}"
    -> no transition

# ── Close ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
from Running on Close
    -> append Log "Campaign closed — {InviteCount} invited, {AcceptedCount} accepted"
    -> transition Closed
